WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.832
[BIRDS CHIRPING]

00:00:01.832 --> 00:00:03.664
[TRAIN ON TRACKS]

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.630
People are feeling romantic
about nationalisation

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:11.350
because they don't
remember how awful it was.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:12.880
The railway is a
natural monopoly.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.250
You don't get to choose
which train you travel on,

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:16.660
which company you choose.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.760
It doesn't make sense
to introduce a market

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:19.980
into the railway.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.750
There are two big problems with
the railways at the moment,

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:24.340
the fares are too
high, and they've

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:25.690
been going up relentlessly.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:28.170
And the second problem
I can put in two words.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:29.060
Southern Rail.

00:00:31.151 --> 00:00:33.400
If you look at some of the
trains they've invested in,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:34.483
they're pretty unpleasant.

00:00:34.483 --> 00:00:35.337
No tables.

00:00:35.337 --> 00:00:36.670
Nowhere to put your drinks down.

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:37.930
It looks like the
sort of place they

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:40.346
want to hose down after the
passengers have been in there.

00:00:40.346 --> 00:00:43.972
I think privatisation has
brought many advances.

00:00:43.972 --> 00:00:45.513
Certainly one of
the advances brought

00:00:45.513 --> 00:00:48.460
is an enormous increase in the
number of people using trains.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.450
Jeremy Corbyn is a
great trainspotter.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.845
So he and I see completely
eye to eye on the railways.

00:00:52.845 --> 00:00:54.470
And I'd love to be
his thin controller.

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.320
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.992
We didn't feel it
appropriate to say

00:00:57.992 --> 00:00:59.950
that the Chancellor and
the Transport Secretary

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:01.870
were locked in mortal combat.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.635
But that is more or
less what it was.

00:01:03.635 --> 00:01:06.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:22.820
Britain is the birthplace
of the modern railway.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:27.390
But the network is now at the
centre of a political battle.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:31.250
25 years ago every train,
every piece of track,

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:35.670
and every station in the UK was
sold off to private companies.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.990
Now, with calls for
re-nationalisation growing

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:43.700
louder, we're here to ask, why
did we privatise the railways?

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:45.200
And was it worth it?

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.230
One man who was
there in the engine

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:53.300
room of the great
Conservative sell off,

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.820
stoking the fires
of privatisation,

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:00.510
is now better known as a
connoisseur of the railways.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.400
Trains were old.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.620
They were very often dirty.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:08.160
And the most famous symbol
was the curly British Rail

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.930
sandwich, because the standard
of catering was so poor.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.479
So it was universally
acknowledged

00:02:12.479 --> 00:02:14.020
that the nationalised
industries were

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:16.830
giving an extremely poor
standard of service,

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:20.250
because they had no
accountability to the customer.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.830
The private railway companies
were taken into public

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:27.090
ownership in 1948, when Clement
Attlee's Labour government

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:30.630
decided to nationalise
swathes of British industry.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:34.650
By the time John Major became
prime minister in 1990,

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.890
the service was seen as a
neglected part of the transport

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.090
system.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:42.030
Ministers thought British
Rail's management was asleep

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:45.450
at the wheel, and looked at ways
to bring in the private sector.

00:02:49.707 --> 00:02:52.880
So why did the
rail privatisation

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.359
come quite so late?

00:02:54.359 --> 00:02:56.150
I think it's partly
because it was complex.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.775
And partly because I don't think
Margaret Thatcher particularly

00:02:58.775 --> 00:02:59.476
wanted to do it.

00:02:59.476 --> 00:03:01.100
I mean, it's odd,
because privatisation

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:05.510
is much associated with Margaret
Thatcher, and rightly so.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.000
Although she moved into
it a little reluctantly.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.940
She was quite slow to do
the first privatisations.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:14.230
And railways and the
post office, for example,

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:15.620
were way down her list.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:18.230
So it only got going
in the Major years.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.450
And even then, it
took a very long time.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:23.530
When I was first working on
privatisation, the plans we had

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:26.630
were not at all the plans
that turned out to be.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:29.600
Quite honestly, the
principal motivation

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.700
was that we had an
industry that we thought

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.780
was in terminal decline.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:38.860
And something, almost anything,
had to be done about it.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.520
And this is really what I would
ask you to judge privatisation

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:42.020
against.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.460
I know there have been
many other changes.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.580
But not many people
gave railways

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:51.100
a hope, even of survival
in the late 1980s.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.030
The railways more than survived.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:56.940
But the journey has
not been smooth.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.610
Awarding contracts at
the lowest possible cost

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.910
has depressed the quality
of train services.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:06.220
The companies complain of
squeezed profit margins.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.690
But passengers still seem
to blame the operators,

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:13.050
as the government piles more of
the costs onto the travelling

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:15.240
public.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:20.230
As passenger fares have
risen, so has public anger.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.610
And the remedy that
these angry commuters

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:26.240
seem to be reaching for is
to take the whole system back

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.520
into public ownership.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.980
One commuter decided
he'd had enough.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:34.100
David Boyle challenged
Southern Rail's poor service

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.800
by organising a protest
at Brighton Station

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.660
with his fellow passengers.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:42.170
They were irate at the delays
and cancellations resulting

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.690
from an ongoing dispute
between the train operating

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:46.880
company and the trade unions.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:48.860
They refused to
show their tickets

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:51.185
and had a tea party instead.

00:04:51.185 --> 00:04:54.096
Well, the reason I got so
cross was during the strike,

00:04:54.096 --> 00:04:55.970
when no one was taking
responsibility for it,

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:58.054
was it felt like you were
going into a restaurant.

00:04:58.054 --> 00:04:59.678
They won't give you
your meal properly.

00:04:59.678 --> 00:05:00.986
They're late in delivering it.

00:05:00.986 --> 00:05:02.360
And they still
expect you to pay.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.192
And what's more, they
check your ticket.

00:05:04.192 --> 00:05:05.900
They have the nerve
to check your ticket.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.560
So it seemed to me,
what would Gandhi

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:08.643
do in those circumstances?

00:05:08.643 --> 00:05:10.207
He would refuse to
show his ticket.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:16.040
It's a ripoff for both
passengers and the public.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:17.150
And it has failed.

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:19.280
That's why we need
public ownership.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.070
Cat Hobbs speaks for We
Own It, a pressure group

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:24.620
campaigning for public
services to be brought back

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:25.950
into government hands.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:29.210
76% of us believe the railways
should be in public ownership.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:30.990
And that's across
all age groups.

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:33.950
We know that people really
believe the railways should

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:34.810
be publicly owned.

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:38.180
And we can achieve
that very easily

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.070
by bringing rail
franchises in house

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:42.110
as they come up for renewal.

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:45.400
When you see the ridiculous
chaos in Southern Railways,

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:49.490
and the huge inconvenience
to the travelling public,

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:51.980
going back several
months, well, of course,

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:54.810
the public are entitled not just
to be annoyed, but to be very,

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:55.310
very angry.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:56.870
If I used Southern
Railway, I'd be

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:59.420
livid at the nonsense
that's gone on there.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:01.340
Another angry protester?

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:04.580
Actually, this is Malcolm
Rifkind, former transport

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:07.100
secretary, and one of the
architects of Britain's

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.480
privatised railways.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.900
I basically reversed
what was perceived

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:16.100
to be a conservative policy
of the previous 12 years,

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:20.090
and said, I am unashamedly
part of a government

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.400
that is committed to an
expansion of the rail network.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.950
And I had John Major's
full support in doing that.

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:28.460
When you were secretary
of state for transport,

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:31.370
you were not in agreement
with the Treasury

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:32.780
on how it should be done.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:33.280
Yes.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:36.710
Although I had no problem with
the principle of privatisation,

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:39.430
where I did depart from
the view of the Chancellor

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:41.990
of the Exchequer
and of the Treasury

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:45.740
was as to the separation
of responsibility

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:48.110
for the infrastructure,
for the railway track,

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:52.280
from the operating of the
railways that use that track.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:55.310
To divorce from the person
who's running the railway

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:58.730
the ability to manage
the track as well

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:01.300
in the most economic
and sensible way

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:06.890
was irrational, bad economics,
and bad business sense.

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.860
Instead of pushing British
Rail into the private sector

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:12.260
as a single company,
it was broken

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:16.280
into three components,
track, rolling stock,

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:17.660
and train operators.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:20.330
The real competition
on the railways

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:21.800
was not from other railways.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.560
It was from whether people
decided to use the rail,

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:27.860
or to use air or
road in order to get

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:29.090
to where they wished to go.

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:32.870
And that meant that by the time
of the 1992 general election,

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:35.030
when the manifesto
was being drawn up,

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:37.220
we had not yet reached
a position on this.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:40.460
This set out in here
is the sort of Britain

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.740
I am going to see, and
lead this country to

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:44.480
in the next few months.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.930
In 1992, a secret
memo was circulated

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:50.300
among senior
conservatives discussing

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.830
how to privatise the railways.

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:56.660
The preferred option was
to approach privatisation

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:59.240
on the basis of line of route.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:07:59.900
Why?

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:02.810
Because this was
easy to explain,

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:06.200
and was appealing to
local regional pride,

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:08.180
and a sense of identity.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:11.240
The memo acknowledges
that this method would not

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:14.690
be the most
pro-competitive solution.

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:17.540
Opinion polls showed
that privatising BR

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:19.830
was popular with the public.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.490
It would be important to
bring about privatisation

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:25.550
in a way that captured
the political trick.

00:08:29.887 --> 00:08:31.720
If it meant you were
adopting a system that,

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:34.120
in all other respects,
was an inferior one, then

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:36.610
that would be a very
bad thing to do.

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:39.490
If you devise a policy
which not only appears

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:42.220
to be the best way of
running the country,

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:45.760
but happens in addition
to be quite popular,

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:47.980
yes, we plead guilty
as politicians

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:50.890
to being attracted to that as
a way of resolving whatever

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:52.090
the problem might be.

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:55.000
But now it seems that the
pendulum of public support has

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:59.200
swung completely in the
other direction and favours

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.150
the Labour solution.

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:05.140
I think the public mood is
there, absolutely there, saying

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:08.200
bring our railways back
into public ownership.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:10.300
Clearly, in your view,
it would be a mistake

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:13.270
to follow the Jeremy Corbyn,
John McDonnell prescription.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:16.717
I think it would be one of the
silliest, most foolish things

00:09:16.717 --> 00:09:17.300
they could do.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:19.150
Apart from anything
else, it would cost

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:20.594
billions of public expenditure.

00:09:20.594 --> 00:09:22.510
There would be massive
disruption for a number

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.599
of years, until you got back.

00:09:24.599 --> 00:09:26.140
And where would you
be going back to?

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:28.270
You'd be going back
to a state system

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:31.810
of running the railways,
which during the period that

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:35.350
had operated in this country saw
a constant decline in railway

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:39.370
usage and a constant increase
in public dissatisfaction

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:41.680
right up till the 1990s
when we privatised.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:44.170
So that's what you call dumb.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:46.090
There's no question
that passenger numbers

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:48.970
have gone up since the
railways were privatised.

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:52.630
The number of journeys has
increased from 761 million

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:57.310
in 1995 to 1.7 billion in 2016.

00:09:57.310 --> 00:10:00.850
But most of this is
concentrated around London.

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:03.520
Not everyone thinks
this is fair.

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:04.650
I'm Lilian Greenwood.

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:06.770
I'm the Labour MP
for Nottingham South.

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:09.930
And I chair the Commons
Transport Select Committee.

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:11.540
We're here on this
train, speeding

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:14.200
north to your constituency
in Nottingham.

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:17.500
And clearly, there's an
enormous amount of investment

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:20.250
that has gone into
the railway system.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:22.000
I mean, you could
almost say, the railways

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:23.760
have never had it so good.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.405
There's a lot more
public money going in now

00:10:26.405 --> 00:10:27.910
than there was at
the time when we

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:31.940
had British Rail as a
nationalised rail industry.

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:33.650
I think that's one
of the things that

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:37.750
perhaps is a concern about
franchising on its own terms.

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:40.690
It was meant to lead to
cheaper fares, a more

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:46.390
efficient railway, requiring
less taxpayer subsidy.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:48.790
And actually, those things
haven't been delivered.

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:51.340
So how much money
does the UK government

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:54.400
spend subsidising each
of the train companies?

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:56.290
Well, it depends.

00:10:56.290 --> 00:10:59.770
Between 2016 and
2017 all of them

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:03.070
received more funds than they
contributed to the government,

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:06.550
with two notable exceptions,
Virgin Trains East

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:09.340
Coast and South West Trains.

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:12.070
South West Trains
lost its franchise.

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:15.055
And Virgin Trains East Coast
has been flirting with collapse.

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:21.080
We ended up taking a
system and throwing it

00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:24.620
into the air with
the infrastructure

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:27.470
pushed off into Railtrack
a privatised infrastructure

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:31.440
company, losing lots of the
engineering and expertise.

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:35.070
We have the train operations put
into 25 different franchises,

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:36.600
many more than there are now.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.340
And, of course,
the rolling stock

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:40.890
sold off to the rolling
stock operators.

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:45.610
So it basically broke
our rail industry up.

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:51.420
I think essentially Railtrack
failed to properly maintain

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:52.040
the railway.

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:54.300
So routine maintenance
wasn't being

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:57.540
done, which led to a
succession of fatal crashes.

00:11:57.540 --> 00:11:59.550
And it was the failure
of a privatised operator

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:01.950
to keep the network
properly maintained

00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:04.399
and to keep people safe that
was an absolute disaster.

00:12:04.399 --> 00:12:06.440
I mean, now we have the
safest railway in Europe.

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:07.650
That's something to be proud of.

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:10.025
But they had to come back into
the public sector for that

00:12:10.025 --> 00:12:11.210
to happen.

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:14.930
Some veterans of the Labour
government in the 2000s who

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:19.160
re-nationalised Rail Track to
create Network Rail now believe

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:23.220
that creating more state backed
bodies could be the answer.

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:25.120
There should be
competition for franchises

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:26.670
between public and
private companies.

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:29.340
After all, we have state
companies in France, Germany,

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:31.040
the Netherlands,
and other countries

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:33.350
who can compete
for contracts here.

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:35.960
What we don't have is domestic
state companies, or not

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:38.186
for profit companies
that can compete.

00:12:38.186 --> 00:12:39.560
There's a lot of
nonsense spoken.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:41.960
Since all of the track and
most of the investment that

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:44.060
goes into the network
is public investment,

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:47.540
we don't have a fully
privatised railway in any event.

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:49.430
Over the last decade,
the private sector

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:51.620
has invested hundreds
of millions of pounds

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:53.780
into the railways each year.

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:55.940
But the amounts
invested by government

00:12:55.940 --> 00:12:59.190
can be measured in
billions yearly.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:01.010
It's not true capitalism, is it?

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:04.570
It's not a true market,
or true competition.

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:06.530
It is, to some extent,
pretend capitalism,

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:09.860
because the railway
operators are always

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:11.180
going to receive subsidy.

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:14.990
So is the supplier of the
track and the infrastructure.

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:16.880
But that isn't the
end of the story.

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:21.500
Because you can have
different franchise seekers

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:23.840
competing with each
other to see whether they

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:28.330
can offer a good standard of
service for a lesser subsidy.

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:30.990
It's a kind of negative
auction, in other words.

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:33.590
They are bidding for
the lowest subsidy.

00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:34.960
Sometimes it goes wrong.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:37.540
But on the whole, it
has not gone wrong.

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:40.370
And those who underperform
can be replaced.

00:13:40.370 --> 00:13:41.090
Will be replaced.

00:13:41.090 --> 00:13:44.589
Have been replaced at the
end of the franchises.

00:13:44.589 --> 00:13:46.880
The reason why there's such
support for nationalisation

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:48.830
is because a lot of private
companies are doing very badly.

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:51.320
They're either not running the
trains, like Southern Rail,

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:54.200
or they're having to be bailed
out at taxpayer expense.

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:55.460
Like the East Coast.

00:13:55.460 --> 00:13:58.370
You cannot justify privatisation
unless the private sector does

00:13:58.370 --> 00:13:59.270
a good job.

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:01.640
I mean, I suppose people
do have short memories.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:03.987
I suppose I'm in a
privileged position,

00:14:03.987 --> 00:14:05.570
because I was in a
privileged position

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:08.760
to remember how bad things were.

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:11.060
Maybe it's the case
that we have to go

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:15.535
through the whole wretched cycle
again and learn the lesson once

00:14:15.535 --> 00:14:16.280
more.

00:14:16.280 --> 00:14:19.430
This nostalgia for
nationalised industries

00:14:19.430 --> 00:14:21.690
is absolutely misplaced.

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:27.170
Perhaps people ought to go
and dig out their old Super 8

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:30.680
movies and try to recall
for themselves what

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:31.570
life was really like.

00:14:35.944 --> 00:14:38.720
I'm doing nothing but wasting
my time the whole of the time

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:39.678
I've been on the train.

00:14:39.678 --> 00:14:41.032
Two hours this morning.

00:14:41.032 --> 00:14:43.270
I never get home till after
half past seven at night.

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:45.686
It didn't arrive down here
until three quarters of an hour

00:14:45.686 --> 00:14:47.760
late, which means
we miss our buses,

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.330
and we don't arrive home until
about an hour and a quarter

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:52.235
later than we normally do.

00:14:52.235 --> 00:14:53.610
Whatever the
solution, there have

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:56.580
been some punch ups between
commuters and railway staff.

00:14:56.580 --> 00:14:58.200
Tempers have flared
on both sides.

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:00.840
And one loco man was
attacked with an umbrella.

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:04.740
There used to be political
energy around the idea

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:06.270
of privatisation.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:10.290
We conservatives are
returning power to the people.

00:15:10.290 --> 00:15:13.300
In the Thatcher and
then the Major era,

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:18.240
privatisation was meant to give
power back to the consumer.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:22.200
But now, perhaps, it means
returning to state control.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:26.467
Let the next Labour government
transform Britain by genuinely

00:15:26.467 --> 00:15:28.175
putting power in the
hands of the people.

00:15:28.175 --> 00:15:29.070
Power to the people.

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:30.445
Power in the hands
of the people.

00:15:30.445 --> 00:15:32.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

